From 79c67bf9d2c99ef62a1d5d38c20e6b7babe36147 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 10:56:32 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Telemetry: write the exit counters with one write and no
 fsync

Every emulated process writes its telemetry counters at exit. The file was
written with one write(2) per counter line and then fsync(2)ed. In a build,
which is hundreds of short processes, that is paid per process: in POWERarm,
which inherited this code, the fsync alone measured 6-16 ms per process on a
disk-backed data directory, and removing it took warm `gcc -c empty.c` from
121.1 to 96.2 ms per invocation.

Format the counters into one buffer, write it once and do not sync. A counter
file lost to a host crash is not worth the cost; the rename still keeps one
backup.

Checked here: 50 runs of a static x86-64 exit_group program, one loop each,
FEX_APP_DATA_LOCATION on tmpfs (where fsync is nearly free):
114.5 -> 112.7 ms per run. The telemetry file is still written in full. The
per-process saving is larger with the default data directory on disk.

Ported from POWERarm abaed2ac0.
---
 FEXCore/Source/Utils/Telemetry.cpp | 9 +++++++--
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/FEXCore/Source/Utils/Telemetry.cpp b/FEXCore/Source/Utils/Telemetry.cpp
index af827a3bb..9b7a0a946 100644
--- a/FEXCore/Source/Utils/Telemetry.cpp
+++ b/FEXCore/Source/Utils/Telemetry.cpp
@@ -95,12 +95,17 @@ void Shutdown(const fextl::string& ApplicationName) {
                                   FEXCore::File::FileModes::WRITE | FEXCore::File::FileModes::CREATE | FEXCore::File::FileModes::TRUNCATE);
 
   if (File.IsValid()) {
+    // One write and no fsync. Every emulated process ends here, and in a build
+    // that is hundreds of short processes: the fsync alone measured 6-16 ms per
+    // process and one write per line 29 syscalls. A counter file lost to a host
+    // crash is not worth that; the rename above still keeps one backup.
+    fextl::string Text;
     for (size_t i = 0; i < TelemetryType::TYPE_LAST; ++i) {
       auto& Name = TelemetryNames.at(i);
       auto& Data = TelemetryValues.at(i);
-      fextl::fmt::print(File, "{}: {}\n", Name, Data.load());
+      Text += fextl::fmt::format("{}: {}\n", Name, Data.load());
     }
-    File.Flush();
+    File.Write(Text);
   }
 }
 
